  "thumbnailAlt": "Creative Brief form beside a photo of a woman pinning photos to a mood board",
  "content": {
    "intro": "A creative brief form is a form that collects the objective, target audience, key message, deliverables, tone and deadline for a piece of design or content work so the creative team can produce the right thing the first time. This template asks for one single-minded message and real reference files, which are the two things most briefs leave vague.",
    "audience": "In-house marketing teams, design studios, content agencies, freelance designers and copywriters, and the account managers who brief them.",
    "useCases": [
      "Requesting a campaign key visual and social cutdowns from an in-house design team",
      "Briefing a freelance copywriter on a product launch email series",
      "Kicking off a logo or packaging redesign with a studio",
      "Standardizing design requests from sales and product teams"
    ],
    "tips": [
      "Share the form as your team's design request link so every job arrives with the same information.",
      "Turn on email notifications so the creative lead sees each new brief and can assign it the same day.",
      "Edit the response with full edit history when scope changes, so everyone can see what was added after kickoff."
    ],
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "What should a creative brief form include?",
        "answer": "The project name and owner, the business objective, the target audience, one key message, supporting proof points, the list of deliverables and sizes, tone of voice, brand mandatories (logo, legal lines, colors), references to follow or avoid, budget and the deadline with review dates."
      },
      {
        "question": "How long should a creative brief be?",
        "answer": "Most good briefs fit on one page. If a requester cannot state the key message in one sentence, the work usually needs more strategy before design starts."
      },
      {
        "question": "Who should fill in the creative brief?",
        "answer": "The person who owns the business goal, such as a marketing manager or account lead. The creative team then reads it, asks questions and agrees it before work begins."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is a single-minded proposition?",
        "answer": "It is the one thing you want the audience to take away, written as a short sentence. Briefs with several equal messages tend to produce cluttered work."
      }
    ]
  },
